# Tests

Tests are the safety net that enables fearless change. A test suite that is slow, fragile, or hard to read provides false confidence and gets disabled. Every test must be an asset, not a liability.

## TEST-01 — Cover happy path and edge cases

Every test suite must cover at minimum:

1. The happy path — valid input produces the expected output.
2. At least one invalid or edge input — missing required field, zero value, boundary value.
3. Any documented error conditions for the function under test.

```
// good
test "createOrder with valid input returns saved order"
test "createOrder with missing customer raises ValidationError"
test "createOrder with zero quantity raises ValidationError"
```

## TEST-02 — One assert per test concept

Each test must verify one logical outcome. Multiple assertions that together verify a single behaviour are acceptable; assertions for distinct behaviours belong in distinct tests. When a test fails, you must immediately know which behaviour broke.

```
// bad — two different behaviours in one test
test "processOrder":
    result = processOrder(validOrder)
    assert result.status == "saved"
    assert emailService.sentCount == 1   // different concern

// good — one behaviour per test
test "processOrder saves the order":
    result = processOrder(validOrder)
    assert result.status == "saved"

test "processOrder sends a confirmation email":
    processOrder(validOrder)
    assert emailService.sentCount == 1
```

## TEST-03 — Use table-driven tests for multiple input/output cases

When testing the same function with multiple input/output combinations, use a table (array of cases) rather than repeating the full test body. This keeps tests concise and makes adding new cases trivial.

```
// good — table-driven
cases = [
    { name: "zero",     input: 0,  want: "",  wantError: true  },
    { name: "positive", input: 5,  want: "5", wantError: false },
    { name: "negative", input: -1, want: "",  wantError: true  },
]
for case in cases:
    test case.name:
        result, error = formatScore(case.input)
        assert (error != null) == case.wantError
        assert result == case.want
```

## TEST-04 — No logic inside test bodies

Test bodies must not contain conditional branches (`if`, `switch`, ternary) that alter what is being asserted. Branching hides untested paths. Use subtests or table entries instead.

```
// bad — conditional hides one of the paths from analysis
result, error = process(input)
if wantError:
    assert error != null
else:
    assert error == null

// good — each case is a dedicated subtest with no branching
test "valid input produces no error":
    _, error = process(validInput)
    assert error == null

test "invalid input returns error":
    _, error = process(invalidInput)
    assert error != null
```

## TEST-05 — Tests must be fast

A test suite that is slow stops being run. Unit tests must run in milliseconds. Integration tests that require real I/O must be clearly separated so the fast suite can run independently.

- Do not make real network calls in unit tests; use stubs or fakes.
- Do not sleep or use real timers; inject controllable time.
- Do not write to the real filesystem; use in-memory alternatives.

## TEST-06 — Tests must be independent

No test may depend on the execution order of other tests or on shared mutable state left by a previous test. Each test sets up its own preconditions and tears down its own state.

```
// bad — test B only passes if test A ran first and left data in the DB
test "A — create user": db.insert(user)
test "B — fetch user": result = db.find(userId) // depends on A

// good — each test owns its setup
test "fetch user":
    db.insert(user)
    result = db.find(userId)
    assert result == user
```

## TEST-07 — Tests must be repeatable

A test must produce the same result on every run in every environment — local, CI, any machine. Tests that fail intermittently (flaky tests) erode trust in the entire suite.

- Seed random values with a fixed seed in tests.
- Stub the clock; never compare against wall-clock time.
- Isolate external services with fakes or contract mocks.

## TEST-08 — Tests must be readable

A test is documentation. Its name and body must communicate what behaviour is being verified and under what conditions, without requiring the reader to understand the implementation.

- Test names describe behaviour, not implementation: `"returns empty list when user has no orders"` not `"test_getOrders_null"`.
- Arrange, Act, Assert: each section is visually distinct.
- No magic literals in assertions — use the same named constants the production code uses.

## TEST-09 — Add backwards-compatibility tests on API changes

When changing a handler, struct, or serialisation format, add or update a test that confirms existing payloads or stored documents still decode correctly. Name the test after the legacy shape so future maintainers understand the constraint.

```
// good
test "legacy document without Tags field decodes to empty slice":
    raw = { id: "123", name: "Widget" }   // Tags absent
    widget = decode(raw)
    assert widget.tags == []
```
